# Cyrius Field Notes — Language Gotchas (platform & ABI)
# Per-OS preprocessor target flags, struct return by value, variadic fns,
# the fncall6-into-extern-C arg-count ceiling, AArch64 cross-`bl`
# register clobber + immediate-encoding limits, and the agnos
# two-sided ABI syscall-wrapper mirror, plus the aarch64 private-alias
# band and the Darwin AT_* flag divergence.
# Refreshed 2026-08-07 at v6.5.10: added the aarch64 ≥1000 private-alias
# band + the Darwin AT_* divergence from v6.5.7 — the release where ONE
# cross-OS test finally RUN on ecb / ach turned up seven wrapper defects
# that five targets' worth of compile-only checks could not see.
# ⚠ v6.6.1: the `vr01_` FILENAME PREFIX this file used to name was RETIRED at
# v6.5.11. The cross-OS selector is now the `tests/tcyr/crossos/` DIRECTORY, so a
# file named `vr01_*` today silently OPTS OUT of the release gate's cross-OS leg —
# the exact opposite of what the prefix used to mean.
# Prior refresh 2026-07-27 at v6.4.82: `fncall6_ceiling_into_extern_c`
# carries a premise-check banner — it predates the v6.4.64 Win64
# stack-arg codegen fix, and PURE cyrius has no arity limit at all
# (5-20 args verified on real hardware). A rule that tells you to
# restructure code around codegen is a bug report, not a rule.
# 15 entries (re-DERIVED at 6.6.14 after three class entries: the drive-relative rooted path
# (CVE-54/57/65 + the DLL search order), SIGPIPE (CVE-66 + the open net.cyr writers), and the
# Winsock socket that is not a file handle — DERIVE with `grep -c "^\[\[entries\]\]"`, do not
# increment; at v6.6.6 the line said 10 while the file held 11, which is why it says DERIVE).

[[entries]]
name = "per_os_target_flags_win_vs_linux"
description = "v5.5.0 introduced CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN vs CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX preprocessor flags for per-OS stdlib selection"
category = "cyrius"
---
v5.5.0 ships `cycc_win` (the Windows cross-compiler) alongside
`cycc` and `cycc_aarch64`. The three cross-entries now predefine
a target-OS flag so stdlib selectors can include the right peer:

    cycc            predefines  CYRIUS_ARCH_X86     + CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX
    cycc_aarch64    predefines  CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64 + CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX
    cycc_win        predefines  CYRIUS_ARCH_X86     + CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN

The two target-OS flags are mutually exclusive. Selectors use
paired `#ifdef` guards rather than `#ifdef` + `#else` (cyrius's
preprocessor has no `#else` / `#ifndef` yet):

    #ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN
    include "lib/syscalls_windows.cyr"
    #endif
    #ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX
    #ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86
    include "lib/syscalls_x86_64_linux.cyr"
    #endif
    #ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64
    include "lib/syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr"
    #endif
    #endif

Only one of the three inner blocks runs per compile. Adding a
new target OS (e.g. BSD) is another predefined flag + another
top-level block in the selector.

When writing stdlib modules that have OS-specific behaviour, use
the same pattern:
    lib/foo.cyr          public dispatch (selector)
    lib/foo_linux.cyr    Linux peer
    lib/foo_windows.cyr  Windows peer

[[entries]]
name = "struct_return_by_value_5_5_36"
description = "`fn make(): Point { ... return p; }` compiles — struct return is native on both SysV and Win64"
category = "cyrius"
---
Pre-v5.5.36 callers had to pass `&Point` out-params; the
compiler didn't wire struct-by-value return for >8B types.
v5.5.36 closed it:

    fn make_point(x: int, y: int): Point {
        stack var p: Point;
        p.x = x;
        p.y = y;
        return p;
    }

    fn caller() {
        var got: Point = make_point(3, 4);  // deep copy
    }

ABI plumbing (automatic):
 - SysV: hidden retptr in RDI (first arg).
 - Win64: hidden retptr in RCX (first arg).
 - Callee memcpys the local into *retptr before RET.

The `var got: Point = make_point(...)` form on the LHS is also
new in v5.5.36 — parser recognises struct-returning call on the
RHS and emits the retptr setup.

Use return-by-value for small packed values (Point, Range,
Result). Long-lived structs should still live on the heap.

[[entries]]
name = "variadic_fn_5_5_36"
description = "`fn printf(fmt: str, ...)` syntax lands in v5.5.36 (Phase 3-min); use lib/varargs.cyr inside the body"
category = "cyrius"
---
v5.5.36 added `...` as the variadic-parameter token:

    fn printf(fmt: str, ...) {
        // use lib/varargs.cyr va_start / va_arg / va_end
    }

`...` is TOK=112, only valid as the last parameter. The compiler
sets a GFVA flag on the callee so call sites know to handle
variadic ABI quirks:
 - Win64 MSDN rule: float args to a variadic callee go into
   BOTH the XMM register file AND the RCX/RDX/R8/R9 GPR slots.
   The compiler emits the duplication at the call site; you
   don't write it.
 - SysV passes float args in XMM0-7 as usual; RAX on the call
   holds the number of XMM regs used, per SysV §3.5.7.

Phase 3-min (shipped v5.5.36) covers primitives-through-varargs.
Phase 3-full (future) expands va_arg to structs-by-value and
nested varargs — for now, if you need those shapes, pass a
pointer or a single `u64` and unpack.

[[entries]]
name = "fncall6_ceiling_into_extern_c"
description = "Cyrius fns that internally fncall* into wgpu-native (or any extern-C) were reported to segfault at 7+ params; pure Cyrius has NO arity limit (5-20 verified v6.4.64). UNVERIFIED since v6.4.64, which fixed a Win64 stack-arg codegen P0 in the same shape — premise-check before adopting the struct-pointer workaround."
category = "cyrius"
---
**PREMISE-CHECK BEFORE ADOPTING THE WORKAROUND (added at the
v6.4.82 closeout).** This entry has not been re-verified since
**v6.4.64**, which fixed a real Win64 codegen P0 in exactly this
shape: `ECALLPOPS`'s PE branch shuttled stack args through a fixed
5-register table with no code path past `nextra == 5`, so a call at
10+ args both mis-popped the register args and emitted NO stack-arg
writes — silently corrupting argument 1. SysV had had the same class
fixed at v5.6.24 / v6.0.57. It is entirely possible that some or all
of the crash described below was that compiler bug rather than an
inherent FFI boundary. Reproduce the 7-arg crash on your current
toolchain before restructuring your API around it — and if it
reproduces, that is a compiler bug to FILE, not a rule to code
around. (This is the language repo: when the compiler can't compile
valid cyrius, fix the compiler.)

---

A Cyrius fn signature CAN take 7+ args without issue when the
body is pure Cyrius — **there is no cyrius arity limit** (5-20 args
verified on real Windows / Linux / aarch64 at v6.4.64). The claim
below is specifically about the extern-C boundary: the moment the
body contains a `fncall*`
into wgpu-native (or any extern-C function table), the whole
signature must stay at ≤ 6 args or the call segfaults at
runtime.

Example shape that crashes:

```cyrius
fn _backend_wgpu_some_op(ctx, a, b, c, d, e, f) {  # 7 args
    fncall6(slot, a, b, c, d, e, f);                # crash
}
```

Same fn at 6 args works. Same fn at 7 args without the inner
fncall* works (pure Cyrius all the way down).

**Why.** The Cyrius calling convention for fns that internally
make C-ABI calls passes args through registers in a way that
doesn't fully line up with the System V x86_64 ABI when the
caller has 7+ args of its own. The first 6 params land in
rdi/rsi/rdx/rcx/r8/r9 cleanly; the 7th would go on the stack,
and the stack alignment / spill protocol differs from how
wgpu-native expects to be called. Result: register clobber that
manifests as a segfault inside the wgpu fn, not at the call
site.

**The fix.** Pack 7+ args into a struct pointer:

```cyrius
fn _backend_wgpu_some_op(ctx, args_ptr) {
    var a = load64(args_ptr + 0);
    var b = load64(args_ptr + 8);
    # ... unpack rest ...
    fncall6(slot, a, b, c, d, e, f);
}
```

Caller side allocates a struct, fills the fields, passes the
ptr. This is the pattern mabda uses for `wgpu_*` ops with 7+
inputs (e.g., `wgpu_command_encoder_copy_buffer_to_buffer`).

**When it doesn't apply.** Pure Cyrius fns with no inner
fncall* into extern-C — those take 12+ args fine. mabda's
internal helpers like `native_pm4_build_render_clear_triangle`
take 6 args and would work at 12 if needed.

**Cross-pollination.** Same constraint will apply to any
Cyrius project that builds a function-table-of-extern-C-fns
abstraction: `samvada` (sd_bus C shim), future
`vidya_logind`-style packages, anything wrapping libc beyond
the stdlib. Document at the boundary; don't trial-and-error.

[[entries]]
name = "aarch64_callee_saved_and_imm_limits"
description = "AArch64 cross-`bl` clobbers x0–x18 (caller-saved); `cmp xN, #imm` is 12-bit unsigned, `mov xN, #imm` is 16-bit — larger values need ldr literal-pool"
category = "cyrius"
---
THREE CONJOINED AArch64 ABI / encoding gotchas, all surfaced
during the v2.3.3 → v2.3.4 vidya backfill. Different in
mechanism, identical in symptom: the assembler accepts the
source, the binary runs, the output is wrong.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
1. Caller-saved vs callee-saved across `bl`
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

AArch64 AAPCS64 reserves 32 GP registers split into roles. The
ones that matter for hand-written asm calling helper fns:

    x0–x7    — argument / return value (caller-saved)
    x8       — indirect result location (caller-saved)
    x9–x15   — temporaries (caller-saved)
    x16, x17 — intra-procedure-call scratch (caller-saved)
    x18      — platform register / TLS scratch (caller-saved)
    x19–x28  — callee-saved (helper preserves on entry, restores on exit)
    x29      — frame pointer
    x30      — link register (return address)

Any value held in x0–x18 across a `bl` to a helper is **assumed
gone** unless the caller spilled it. The helper is free to
clobber every one of those registers — and stdlib helpers
DO clobber them, even when their visible job is "compute one
small thing."

Two rescue patterns:

  (a) Cache loop state in callee-saved registers x19–x28:

      stp x29, x30, [sp, #-16]!
      stp x19, x20, [sp, #-16]!     # save callee-saveds before use
      mov x19, x0                    # cache loop counter
      mov x20, x1                    # cache base ptr
    .L1:
      mov x0, x19                    # restore from cache
      bl  helper                     # x0–x18 clobbered, x19/x20 fine
      add x19, x19, #1
      cmp x19, #100
      b.lt .L1
      ldp x19, x20, [sp], #16
      ldp x29, x30, [sp], #16
      ret

      The `stp/ldp` pair before/after the loop is mandatory —
      the helper expects to find x19/x20 unchanged when *it*
      returns to ITS caller, which is your function returning
      to ITS caller. Forgetting the spill turns a clean local
      bug into a callee-clobber that surfaces several frames
      up.

  (b) Recompute after each call, don't cache:

      mov x0, x9                     # x9 holds idx
      bl  helper                     # x9 may be clobbered now
      ldr x9, [sp, #16]              # reload idx from frame
      add x9, x9, #1
      str x9, [sp, #16]
      ...

      Slower (one ldr/str per iteration vs free register reads)
      but doesn't require setting up a callee-saved spill at
      function entry. Use when the loop is shallow or the
      cached value is only read once per call.

WHERE THIS BIT US:

  v2.3.3  grid_pathfinding/asm_x86_64.s — A* helper called
          `manhattan` via bl; the outer loop's row/col indices
          lived in caller-saved regs and got clobbered. Same
          symptom on AArch64 sibling. Caught by the BFS-vs-A*
          path-length agreement test.
  v2.3.3  maze_generation/asm_x86_64.s — `idx` register
          clobbered across the PCG `rng_next` bl. PCG output
          sequence diverged from the reference seed=42 trace.
  v2.3.4  btree_indexing/asm_aarch64.s — node-walk loop cached
          parent pointer in x10 across `bl key_cmp`. Tree
          walk corrupted on first split. 3rd surfacing.
  v2.3.4  sql_parsing/asm_aarch64.s — token-stream loop cached
          token index in x12. SELECT validator silently
          accepted malformed input. 4th surfacing.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
2. `cmp xN, #imm` is 12-bit unsigned
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

The immediate field of `cmp xN, #imm` (and `subs xzr, xN, #imm`,
which is what `cmp` is) is 12 bits unsigned, with an optional
LSL #12 shift. So the legal range without shift is 0–4095, and
with the shift it's 4096–16777215 in steps of 4096 — useful for
page boundaries, useless for arbitrary numbers.

    cmp x0, #4095          # OK
    cmp x0, #4096          # ERROR: immediate out of range
    cmp x0, #4166          # ERROR (DT_US/4 = 4166)

CORRECT SHAPE — load the immediate into a temp register, then
compare register-to-register:

    ldr x16, =4166         # literal pool (assembler emits an
                           # ldr from a PC-relative pool slot)
    cmp x0, x16            # OK — register form has no immediate

`x16` (IP0) is the canonical scratch register for this — it's
caller-saved AND reserved for intra-procedure-call use, so the
linker won't repurpose it for veneer insertion.

WHERE THIS BIT US:

  v2.3.3  game_loop_architecture/asm_aarch64.s — fixed-timestep
          accumulator compared against `DT_US/4 = 4166`. The
          assembler error was clear ("immediate out of range")
          but the fix wasn't obvious — the natural reflex is
          "use shift," which doesn't apply here.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
3. `mov xN, #imm` is 16-bit
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

`mov xN, #imm` is a synthetic instruction that the assembler
expands to `movz` (move with zero) or `movn` (move with NOT).
Both encodings carry a 16-bit immediate plus a 2-bit shift
selector (LSL by 0 / 16 / 32 / 48). So a single `mov` covers
any 16-bit value, plus 16-bit values shifted to any quarter of
the 64-bit word.

For a fully arbitrary 64-bit constant, the assembler chains
`movz` + up to three `movk` (move with keep) instructions,
each filling one 16-bit slot. Most assemblers do this expansion
silently when you write `mov xN, #imm` — but the failure mode
when the value can't be expressed cleanly is, again, "immediate
out of range."

    mov x0, #65535         # OK — single movz
    mov x0, #65536         # OK — single movz, LSL #16
    mov x0, #100000        # error or silent-wrong (depends on as)
    mov x0, #1442695040888963407   # error — needs 4-instr chain

CORRECT SHAPE — same rescue as `cmp`: literal pool + ldr.

    ldr x0, =1442695040888963407   # PCG64 default-stream
                                   # increment; one instruction,
                                   # one 8-byte pool slot

The assembler chooses between mov-chain (no pool, 4 instructions,
no memory access) and ldr-from-pool (1 instruction, 1 memory
load) based on the constant's shape. For PCG-style 64-bit
multipliers and ChaCha-style state words, ldr-from-pool always
wins.

WHERE THIS BIT US:

  v2.3.3  maze_generation/asm_aarch64.s — PCG multiplier
          0x5851F42D4C957F2D loaded via mov, only the bottom
          16 bits actually landed. Maze generation produced
          deterministic output, but the wrong deterministic
          output. Caught by the cross-language byte-parity
          assertion (`cells[0]=13, cells[27]=12, cells[63]=6`)
          when the AArch64 port disagreed with all 9 other
          ports.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
The pattern across all three
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

AArch64's encoding-tight ISA + the AAPCS64 split means writing
asm for it differs from x86_64 in three predictable ways:

  - You can't dump arbitrary 64-bit constants into instructions.
    Use the literal pool (`ldr xN, =value`) by reflex when the
    constant exceeds 16 bits.
  - You can't park values in caller-saved regs across a `bl`.
    Decide once per function: callee-saved (set up a spill at
    entry, pay one stp + one ldp) OR memory (one ldr/str per
    use, slower but no setup).
  - The assembler error messages are mostly accurate, but they
    don't suggest the literal-pool fix. The reflex is to add a
    shift; the literal pool is the right answer ~90% of the
    time.

x86_64 hides all three: arbitrary 64-bit immediates fit in
`movabs`, caller-saved registers are fewer (rax, rcx, rdx, rsi,
rdi, r8–r11), so the surface area of "what can I keep across a
call" is smaller and more obvious. Porting an x86_64 asm
example to aarch64 always surfaces at least one of these three.

[[entries]]
name = "agnos_two_sided_abi_mirror"
description = "An `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS` syscall-wrapper band is byte-identical on every non-agnos target; verify agnos-only wrappers via agnos-crossbuild-gate.sh + a call-probe. The a4=r10 5-arg syscall form rides r10 (push→pop)."
category = "cyrius"
---
**v6.4.2** — the agnos audio syscall band (`sys_snd_*`, #64–#69) landed as the ring-3 half of a two-sided ABI (kernel HDA handlers land in parallel on the agnos arm). The pattern for adding an agnos syscall wrapper, and why it's low-risk to the cross-OS self-host:

THE TWO-SIDED MIRROR. `lib/syscalls_x86_64_agnos.cyr` is a STANDALONE faithful mirror of `agnos/docs/development/agnos-userland-abi.md` — it does NOT include `syscalls_linux_common.cyr`. agnos has its own numbers, exit=0 (not Linux 60), AO_* open flags (not Linux O_*), and the explicit-length invariant (every path arg carries its length — no NUL assumption). The register convention IS the same as x86_64-Linux (rax=num, rdi/rsi/rdx/r10 = a1..a4), so the `syscall` instruction EMIT is unchanged — only the NUMBERS differ. RE-FREEZE RULE (§5): the kernel doc is canonical; this file mirrors it. If a number/signature changes in the doc, re-freeze here.

BYTE-IDENTICAL EVERYWHERE ELSE. The wrappers are `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS`-gated (the flag is predefined only for the agnos emit target, `CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS=1`; `src/main.cyr` reads it early). So they're #ifdef'd OUT of every non-agnos build → cycc self-hosts byte-identical on Linux/macOS/Windows/aarch64, and seed → cybs → cycc stays byte-identical. A pure-additive agnos wrapper band is NOT a compiler change.

HOW TO VERIFY AGNOS-ONLY CODE (a Linux-hosted cycc can't run it). Two gates, both host-side: (1) the standing `scripts/agnos-crossbuild-gate.sh` (9 sub-checks → a valid agnos ELF) must stay green; (2) a CALL-PROBE — a tiny program that calls every new wrapper, compiled with `build/cyrius build --agnos`, must produce a valid agnos ELF (rc=0, no reachable-undefined fn) and emit `syscall` with the right `eax` numbers. Runtime (HDA → ALC897 for audio) validates QEMU-side on the agnos arm, not here.

THE a4=r10 5-ARG FORM. agnos's 4-arg syscalls pass the 4th arg in r10 (§1a). In Cyrius source that's a 5-arg `syscall(N, a1, a2, a3, a4)` call — e.g. `sys_snd_write_nb(slot,buf,frames)` is `syscall(SYS_SND_WRITE, slot, buf, frames, 1)` where the trailing `1` = O_NONBLOCK bit0. The compiler passes that 4th syscall arg through **push → pop %r10**, so the non-blocking wrapper shares the exact instruction shape of the blocking form (`sys_symlink#63` uses the same 5-arg form) — no ABI drift between them.

The band: `SND_OPEN=64, SND_CONFIG=65, SND_WRITE=66, SND_CLOSE=67, SND_DRAIN=68, SND_AVAIL=69`; seven wrappers (write + write_nb share #66). Frozen ABI is a single 48000 Hz / 16-bit / stereo S16-interleaved output stream, slot 0..3, and #65–#69 reject a slot not owned by the caller (-1).


[[entries]]
name = "sys_exit_is_per_thread_use_sys_exit_group"
description = "`sys_exit` is exit(2) and ends only the CALLING THREAD — a threaded program's epilogue must call `sys_exit_group` (v6.5.6, all 3 peer families). SYS_EXIT_GROUP is absent on agnos, unrouted on PE, and was an untranslated SIGSYS on macOS-x86."
category = "cyrius"
---
**v6.5.6** — filed by agnosai after a ported server would not terminate. `sys_exit` wraps **exit(2)**, which ends only the calling thread. In a single-threaded program that ends the process, so the idiomatic epilogue looked correct for years:

    var r = main();
    syscall(SYS_EXIT, r);       # ends ONE thread

The moment the program spawns its first worker — `thread_create`, a pooled server, any `chan_*` fan-out — the same line stops terminating the process: the main thread dies, the workers keep running, and it **hangs with no diagnostic**. Measured, one token apart: `syscall(SYS_EXIT, r)` → timed out (124); `sys_exit_group(r)` → 42.

WHY IT SURVIVED SO LONG. `cyrius init` shipped that epilogue in **seven** templates, so it was the default a consumer copied and then stopped looking at — and the failure only appears long after the epilogue was written, when the program grows a thread. The bug is in the *interaction*, not the line, which is why nothing flagged it.

WHY THE WRAPPER NEEDED THREE DEFINITIONS, NOT ONE. `lib/syscalls_linux_common.cyr` is included by only three peers — Linux-x86, Linux-aarch64, and **macOS-x86** (`syscalls_macos.cyr:306`). Windows and agnos are STANDALONE. A single definition there would have left `sys_exit_group` undefined on two targets, so a portable epilogue could not name it:

    syscalls_linux_common  -> SYS_EXIT_GROUP   231 x86 / 94 aarch64
    syscalls_windows       -> SYS_EXIT         ExitProcess ALREADY ends every thread;
                                               231 has no PE reroute and would emit a
                                               raw 0F 05 (illegal instruction)
    syscalls_x86_64_agnos  -> SYS_EXIT         agnos defines no exit_group; #0 tears
                                               down the whole proc

macOS-arm64 needs nothing: `lib/syscalls.cyr` routes it to the **aarch64** peer, so it resolves 94, and `src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr` already maps `94 -> BSD 1`.

THE HOLE THIS EXPOSED. macOS-**x86** resolves `SYS_EXIT_GROUP` to 231 and `EMACHO_SYSXLAT` had **no 231 entry**, so the raw number reached Darwin *unclassed* (class 0 = `SYSCALL_CLASS_NONE`) and the kernel killed the process with SIGSYS. It was silent at compile time as well: the "syscall not routed" warning is gated on `_TARGET_MACHO == 2`, i.e. **arm64 only**, so an x86-Mach-O build compiled clean and died at runtime. Fixed with one table entry (`231 -> 0x2000001`) beside the existing `60 -> 1`. The general lesson: a constant being *defined* on a target does not mean it is *routed* there — for Mach-O and PE, grep the translation table, not the enum.

Gate: `tests/exit_group_wrapper.sh`. Its axis 2 asserts the OLD epilogue **still hangs** — without that, axis 1 would silently stop proving anything the day the premise changed.

[[entries]]
name = "raw_syscall_numbers_on_aarch64_and_the_darwin_at_flag_divergence"
description = "On aarch64, a raw `syscall(N, …)` is NOT the number the kernel sees — ESYSXLAT rewrites a band of x86-compat numbers, so the native `chdir` (49) ran `bind` and the native `fchownat` (54) ran `setsockopt`. v6.5.7 opened a cyrius-private alias band at **≥1000** (`1000 + native number`) for the cases where BOTH the native and the x86 number are already owned. Separately, the `AT_*` FLAGS diverge on Darwin — only `AT_FDCWD` had ever been corrected — so a Linux-valued flag reaching Darwin is simply invalid: EINVAL, forever, silently."
category = "cyrius"
---
**v6.5.7.** Two distinct traps, found the same way: by adding ONE
`syscall_wrappers.tcyr` to the release gate's cross-OS leg so the
wrappers were finally RUN on ecb / ach instead of only compiled.
⚠ It lives at `tests/tcyr/crossos/syscall_wrappers.tcyr` — the `vr01_` prefix
this entry originally named was retired at v6.5.11 and the DIRECTORY is now the
selector; a `vr01_`-named file is no longer picked up at all.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
1. A raw `syscall(N, …)` on aarch64 is not the number the kernel sees
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

`ESYSXLAT` (`src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr`) rewrites a chain of
x86-compat source numbers so that 51 ecosystem repos emitting raw
`syscall(54, …)` and friends keep working on ARM. The consequence is
that some **aarch64-NATIVE** numbers are already spoken for as x86
source numbers, and a native-numbered call is silently re-aimed:

    source 49  → 200   # x86-compat bind      — so native chdir(49)     ran bind
    source 54  → 208   # x86-compat setsockopt— so native fchownat(54)  ran setsockopt

The documented escape has always been "use the OTHER architecture's
number plus a pure-renumber ESYSXLAT row". For these two there was no
number left to borrow: x86's 80 is this peer's own `SYS_FSTAT`, and
x86's 260 is its own `SYS_WAIT4` (native, must pass through
untranslated).

THE ANSWER — **the private alias band.** Source numbers **≥1000** are
cyrius-private aliases no OS will ever mint (Linux is in the 400s and
grows ~5/yr; Darwin is routed separately), renumbered to their true
target by ESYSXLAT. Convention is `1000 + the native aarch64 number`,
so the alias documents itself:

    SYS_FCHOWNAT = 1054     # → 54 on aarch64-Linux, → 468 on Darwin
    SYS_CHDIR    = 1049     # → 49 on aarch64-Linux, → 12  on Darwin

⛔ **Adding an alias needs THREE edits, not one**: an ESYSXLAT arm on
the aarch64-**Linux** branch, one on the **macho** branch, AND a
`_macho_arm_routes` row — because macOS-arm64 resolves this same peer.
Two out of three is a stale-`x16` garbage call.

⚠ **The aarch64-Linux arm MUST sit LAST in its chain.** It produces
`x8 = 54`, and the `setsockopt` entry earlier in that chain compares
against 54 and would re-catch it — every `fchownat` silently reissued
as `setsockopt`. Mutation-verified: the misordered build returns
`ENOPROTOOPT`. Same ordering trap already documented on flock / poll /
epoll_wait.

PRACTICAL RULE FOR CONSUMERS: **do not emit raw `syscall(N, …)` with a
number you read off a Linux man page.** Use the named `SYS_*` constant
from the peer, or a `sys_*` wrapper. The constant is the only thing
that knows about the translation chain — and note the failure mode is
never a compile error and never a signal, just a different syscall
returning a plausible errno.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
2. The `AT_*` flags diverge on Darwin — and only the dirfd was fixed
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

`AT_FDCWD` was corrected to **-2** on macOS when ports found it. The
FLAGS were left Linux-valued for another two-and-a-half minors. Live
values (`lib/syscalls_linux_common.cyr`, read off Darwin's
`sys/fcntl.h` on ecb):

    constant              Darwin   Linux
    AT_FDCWD                  -2    -100
    AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW     0x20   0x100
    AT_SYMLINK_FOLLOW       0x40   0x400
    AT_REMOVEDIR            0x80   0x200
    AT_EMPTY_PATH          (none)  0x1000   ← Darwin has NO equivalent

The at-family entries are **pure renumbers** — they pass their flags
straight through — so a Linux value on Darwin is not "wrong-ish", it is
an invalid flag: EINVAL, no warning, no fault, forever. Measured on
real ecb: `xrmdir` had **never** worked on macOS-arm64 since it shipped
at v6.5.2, and `sys_fchownat` returned -22 the first time it was ever
run there.

⭐ **THE THROUGH-LINE, worth more than either fact.** Five of the seven
defects that release were **half-fixes that stopped at the first
symptom**: `AT_FDCWD` without its flags; `STAT_SIZE` corrected without
its sibling offsets (so every `STAT_MODE` test on Intel-Mac was
silently false, reading the low half of `st_ino`); `unlink` mapped
without `rmdir`, which is the SAME syscall distinguished only by
`AT_REMOVEDIR`; three of four link syscalls mapped. Each looked
finished because the thing that prompted it started working. When you
fix one constant or one route in a family, fix the family.

AND THE COMPANION RULE, from the entry above: **a constant being
DEFINED on a target does not mean it is ROUTED there.** For Mach-O and
PE, grep the translation table, not the enum. The "syscall not routed"
warning is gated on `_TARGET_MACHO == 2` — arm64 only — so an
x86-Mach-O build compiles clean and dies at runtime.

[[entries]]
name = "the_stdlib_now_names_the_send_side_truncate_memfd_and_the_scheduler_pair"
description = "v6.6.5. sys_sendmsg / sys_sendto / sys_ftruncate / sys_truncate / sys_memfd_create / sys_nanosleep / sys_sched_yield exist in lib/syscalls.cyr on every target. Before them a consumer had to hardcode the x86_64 number, and on ELF-aarch64 that runs a DIFFERENT, VALID syscall with a clean, silent build — raw 77 ran tee, raw 46 ran ftruncate, raw 44 ran fstatfs."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["syscalls", "aarch64", "esysxlat", "portability", "wayland", "shm", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
**WHAT TO WRITE.** The names, always:

    var fd = sys_memfd_create("buf", 1);        # MFD_CLOEXEC; -78 on macOS, < 0 on Windows
    sys_ftruncate(fd, w * h * 4);
    sys_sendmsg(sock, &msghdr, 0);              # SCM_RIGHTS fd-passing
    sys_sendto(sock, buf, n, 0, &addr, alen);
    sys_truncate(path, len);
    sys_nanosleep(&timespec, 0);                # PE -> Sleep, macOS -> poll, agnos -> sleep_ms
    sys_sched_yield();

⛔ **WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN AN ERGONOMIC WIN.** ELF-aarch64 passes an unrecognised syscall
number straight to the `svc` — it has to, so native numbers survive. So the x86 number is not
an error there, it is a different call. Measured under `qemu-aarch64 -strace` before v6.6.5:

| you wrote | you meant | aarch64 ran |
|---|---|---|
| 319 | memfd_create | ENOSYS |
| 77 | ftruncate | **tee** |
| 46 | sendmsg | **ftruncate** |
| 44 | sendto | fstatfs |
| 35 | nanosleep | unlinkat(0, NULL, 0) |
| 87 / 84 / 83 / 89 / 33 | unlink / rmdir / mkdir / readlink / dup2 | timerfd_gettime / sync_file_range / fdatasync / acct / mknodat |

v6.6.5 routes all of those, so the x86 spelling now WORKS as well — but the name is still the
answer, because six numbers stay unrouted on purpose: **24** (`sched_yield` on x86 is **dup3**
on aarch64), 53 (socketpair / fchmodat), 52 (getpeername / fchmod), 21 (access / epoll_ctl),
90 (chmod / capget) and 17 (pread64 / getcwd). A compat row for any of those would break the
native call somebody already uses.

⚠ **DO NOT WRITE THE AARCH64-NATIVE NUMBER UNDER AN `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64`.** It looks
like the careful thing and it is the fragile one: a routing row matches a NUMBER and cannot
tell your native number from the x86 number it is chasing. Three ecosystem repos had exactly
that shape when v6.6.5 landed and all three break at their pin bump — thoth's `= 46` (meant as
native ftruncate) becomes sendmsg, kybernet's `= 45` (truncate) becomes recvfrom, and attn11's
`n = 83` (fdatasync) becomes mkdirat. ⚠ A **variable** number is rewritten too: the chain runs
on whatever is in `x8` at run time, so `var n = 83; syscall(n, fd);` is not an escape.

**OFF-LINUX, the wrappers do the right thing rather than the same thing.** memfd declines with
-78 on macOS (Darwin has no memfd_create; shm_open is a different object model) and < 0 on
Windows. nanosleep and sched_yield compose from `poll` on macOS, route to kernel32 `Sleep` on
PE, and use `sleep_ms` on agnos. The agnos peer declares **no Linux constants at all** for
these: #44 is its sched_yield, #46 time_unix, #47 sock_connect, #76 blk_open and #77 blk_read,
so a minted `SYS_FTRUNCATE = 77` there would issue a raw block-device read.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "the_clock_you_get_per_target_is_not_the_clock_you_asked_for"
description = "6.6.5. Darwin CLOCK_MONOTONIC (id 6) is gettimeofday minus boottime with MICROSECOND resolution; Windows had only GetTickCount64's 15 ms tick until QPC; AGNOS has no syscall 228 at all. Three targets where a 'monotonic nanosecond clock' was not one."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["platform", "clock", "macos", "windows", "agnos", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
`now_ns()` and `clock_now_ns()` look portable and were not. Measured per target:

  **macOS.** Passing Darwin clock id **6** (`CLOCK_MONOTONIC`) gets you
  `gettimeofday - boottime` in Apple's own Libc (`gen/clock_gettime.c`), whose `clock_getres` is
  `NSEC_PER_USEC` — **microsecond** resolution. That is exactly the 1,000 ns tick recorded on
  both ecb and ach for a year. Use **4** (`CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW` → `mach_continuous_time`), which
  `lib/sakshi.cyr` and `src/backend/common/runtime.cyr`'s `_prof_clock_ns` already did. The
  arm64 route RETURNS ns in the return register and does NOT fill the timespec; x86-macOS drops
  the id entirely and composes µs from `gettimeofday`, a documented platform ceiling.

  **Windows.** `syscall(228)` routed to kernel32!**GetTickCount64**, whose counter advances in
  **15 ms** steps (measured on real cass, not quoted). Every duration under 15 ms read as 0 or
  as one whole tick — so no cyrius program could time anything on Windows, and the bench
  framework's floor calibrated to 0 because 1,024 calls do not move the counter. 6.6.5 adds the
  PE reroutes **0xF038 / 0xF039** (QueryPerformanceCounter / Frequency) and
  `sys_qpc_ns()`; GetTickCount64 remains as the fallback and as an INDEPENDENT clock for tests.

  **AGNOS.** There is no syscall 228. A build emitted a raw `movl $0xe4,%eax; syscall` into a
  kernel that does not define it, so the timespec read back was undefined. Use `#95`
  `sys_uptime_us` (rdtsc-derived, needs no interrupts — `#40 uptime_ms` is FROZEN in a
  foreground program because IF is cleared).

⚠ **A COST and a TICK are different numbers, and on three of five targets the tick is larger.**
Measured: linux 1,332 ns cost / 1,397 ns tick; ecb 11-39 / 1,000 (pre-fix); ach 540-571 / 1,000;
pi 3,691 / 3,723; cass 0 / 15,000,000. Anything sizing a measurement window from the cost alone
sizes for nothing on a coarse counter.

⭐ **And the general lesson is about the SECOND caller.** `lib/chrono.cyr` was moved to `#95` at
6.6.1; `lib/bench.cyr` has the same `now_ns` and was not, for four releases. A correction applied
to one clock caller is not a correction. Both are now pinned on one gate axis
(`tests/gates/platform/agnos_monotonic_clock_rdtsc.sh`), with an objdump emission check and the
Linux build as the anti-vacuous control.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "routing_a_syscall_number_is_only_half_of_portability_the_struct_width_is_the_other_half"
description = "6.6.6. Every syscall-portability note in this repo is about the NUMBER. statfs showed the second half: Darwin's f_bsize is uint32 with f_iosize packed above it while Linux's is a full 8 bytes, so a shared offset table is still wrong — load64 returns f_bsize | (f_iosize << 32)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["platform", "syscall", "esysxlat", "macos", "aarch64", "statfs", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
The `statfs` repair at 6.6.6 had the familiar shape on the way in — aarch64-Linux's native
`statfs` is **43**, ESYSXLAT's x86-compat row `accept 43→202` was reissuing it, and under
`qemu-aarch64 -strace` a `syscall(43, "/", &buf)` traced as
`accept(6293449, 0x7f…, [0]) = -1 errno=14`. That part is the documented shadow class: declare
the OTHER arch's number and add a row (137→43), below the row that claims your product.

⭐ **The part that was NOT documented anywhere is the struct.** Getting the number right lands
you in a buffer whose layout differs per OS — and this repo already knew about differing
OFFSETS (`STAT_MODE` is +24 on x86_64-Linux, +16 on aarch64-Linux, +4 on Darwin arm64). What
`statfs` adds is a differing **WIDTH**:

  Linux `struct statfs` (both arches, 120 B):  f_type@0(8)  f_bsize@8(8)   f_blocks@16 …
  Darwin `struct statfs64`      (2168 B):      f_bsize@0(**4**)  f_iosize@4(4)  f_blocks@8 …

So `load64(buf + STATFS_BSIZE)` on macOS returns `f_bsize | (f_iosize << 32)` —
**4503599627374592** for a 4096-byte block on a 1 MiB-iosize volume, read off ecb. A caller
multiplies that by a block count and reports a capacity. It never faults, never errors, and
every offset in the table was *correct*.

The answer is an **accessor, not a constant**: peers publish `statfs_bsize(buf)`, which is
`load32` on Darwin and `load64` elsewhere. When a field's width differs across peers, an offset
enum cannot express it and a comment will not stop anyone.

⚠ Two smaller lessons from the same bite, both about notes going stale rather than being wrong:

  * The agnos peer had written an **honest gap** note at v6.5.37 — "`sys_statfs` exists NOWHERE
    else in this repo … the alternative was to invent statfs numbers for four more targets from
    memory … an honest gap beats a plausible wrong number" — and recorded the upgrade path to
    take when a cross-platform consumer appeared. The reasoning was right and the note became
    FALSE the moment the consumer landed. An honest-gap note needs its trigger written into it,
    and someone has to notice the trigger fired: yukti had been the consumer all along, silently
    broken on ARM for four minors.
  * A syscall with **no NAME in the peers** is invisible to the raw-literal diagnostic, because
    that table is derived from names declared in BOTH peers. So "nobody has named it yet" is not
    a neutral state — it is the state in which consumers hardcode numbers and nothing warns.
    Naming the call is the fix; the ESYSXLAT row is only what makes the name correct.

Numbers, all MEASURED rather than recalled (the rule that note demanded): Linux 137/138 x86_64,
43/44 aarch64; Darwin **statfs64 345 / fstatfs64 346**, live-probed on ecb and ach against libc
(the legacy `statfs(157)` fills a different, shifted struct); Windows has none, so the wrapper
declines with -38. Pinned by `tests/gates/platform/statfs_family_routed.sh` and
`tests/tcyr/crossos/statfs_family.tcyr`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "pollout_promises_pipe_buf_not_the_whole_write"
description = "6.6.6. A blocking write larger than the pipe does not come back short — it waits until every byte is delivered — so poll(POLLOUT) then write(n) blocks on byte 65537 exactly as an unpolled write does. POLLOUT on a pipe promises PIPE_BUF (4096) bytes of room and nothing more."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["platform", "pipe", "poll", "syscall", "deadline", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
Bounding a pump that feeds a child's stdin looks like this, and it is wrong:

    if (_poll_fd(fd, 4, timeout_ms) <= 0) { return written; }   # POLLOUT
    var w = sys_write(fd, buf + written, n - written);          # ... the whole remainder

It was measured RED in 6.6.6 with a 1 MB source piped into a child that never reads: the
poll returns ready (the pipe is empty), the write fills the 64 KB buffer and then **blocks
for ever**, because a blocking `write(2)` of more than the pipe can hold does not return a
short count — it waits until the whole request is delivered. A short count comes back only
when a signal handler interrupts a write that has already transferred something.

What POLLOUT actually promises on a pipe is that a write of up to **PIPE_BUF (4096)** bytes
will not block. So the chunk is the bound:

    var chunk_cap = n;
    if (timeout_ms > 0) { chunk_cap = 4096; }   # PIPE_BUF — what POLLOUT promises
    ... var chunk = n - written; if (chunk > chunk_cap) { chunk = chunk_cap; }

The READ side does not need this: `read(2)` returns as soon as one byte is available, so a
poll-then-read of any size cannot block.

Two more things the same bite measured, worth having before you write one of these:

  * **The deadline must be on the PUMP as well as the WAIT.** Every fork+exec verb in
    `lib/regression.cyr`, `lib/process.cyr` and `programs/checks/` pumps the child's pipe
    BEFORE it waits, so a wait deadline alone never runs — the runner is already blocked in
    `sys_read`/`sys_write`. `tests/gates/toolchain/check_driver_bounded.sh` axis 2d pins it.
  * **Writing into a child that already exited kills the WRITER with SIGPIPE**, default
    disposition, no diagnostic — exit 141 from a runner that looks like it crashed. That is
    unrelated to the deadline and is still open in this tree (pre-6.6.6 behaviour); a pump
    with no SIGPIPE handling reports a dead child as its own death. One instance of the class
    in `a_write_to_a_reset_peer_raises_sigpipe_and_a_library_may_neither_rely_on_nor_change_the_disposition`.

`syscall(7, &pfd, 1, ms)` is the portable spelling: the backend translates 7 to Darwin poll
(230) and to aarch64 `ppoll` (73) with the ms->timespec shift, which is why `lib/net.cyr`
has used that literal since v6.2.10.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_rooted_posix_path_is_drive_relative_on_windows_and_any_local_user_can_plant_it"
description = "CLASS — FOUR CVEs in five releases (6.6.11 to 6.6.15), each found one module at a time. On Windows a rooted path such as /etc/hosts resolves against the CURRENT drive (C:\\etc\\hosts), and any authenticated user may create a folder at the root of the system drive, so a stdlib that opens a POSIX path on every target lets one local user answer every other user's program. CVE-54 (6.6.11, hosts + resolv.conf), CVE-57 (6.6.12, sandhi's resolver), CVE-65 (6.6.14, the TLS trust store), CVE-73 (6.6.15, sigil's TPM / Secure Boot / IMA / dm-verity / LUKS helpers — a planted file answered trust questions and received a LUKS key). Two of the four were in FOLDED stdlib. The loader's DLL search order is the same planting, one level down"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["platform", "windows", "pe", "security", "path-planting", "dll-search-order", "crypt32", "callptr", "sigil", "tpm", "fail-closed", "CVE-54", "CVE-57", "CVE-65", "CVE-73", "v6.6.11", "v6.6.12", "v6.6.14", "v6.6.15"]
---
**THE MECHANISM.** `file_open` hands a rooted path to `CreateFileW`, and Windows resolves
`\etc\...` against the current drive — `C:\etc\ssl\cert.pem` for a program started on C:.
Creating `C:\etc` needs no privilege. Nothing fails and nothing warns: the file is absent until
someone plants it, and from then on it is trusted.

| CVE | release | opened on Windows | what the planter controlled |
|---|---|---|---|
| 54 | 6.6.11 | `/etc/hosts`, `/etc/resolv.conf` (`lib/net.cyr`), plus a fallback nameserver 127.0.0.1:53 — port 53 is not privileged on Windows | every other user's name resolution |
| 57 | 6.6.12 | `/etc/resolv.conf` (the folded sandhi resolver) | every other user's DNS server |
| 65 | 6.6.14 | `/etc/ssl/cert.pem` + three sibling bundles (`_tn_ca_read`) | every other user's TLS trust anchors — measured on cass, a planted `cert.pem` became the ONLY root |
| 73 | 6.6.15 (sigil 3.13.9) | `/dev/tpmrm0`, the IMA securityfs files, `/sys/module/dm_verity`, `/usr/bin/mokutil`, a `/tmp` staging dir | "is there a TPM", "what did IMA measure" (and `ima_write_policy` WROTE into the plant), "is dm-verity available" — and `luks_write_keyfile` staged a LUKS key in a directory the planter owns |

**CVE-73 was listed here as "still open" at 6.6.14 — for the TPM helpers only.** The fix found
the class in EVERY sigil trust core. Its shape is the reusable one when a POSIX mechanism has no
Windows equivalent at all: one predicate (`agnosys_rooted_paths_untrusted()`, 1 on Windows) asked
before every probe of a fixed rooted path, and each probe FAILS CLOSED — `tpm_detect` 0,
`secureboot_detect_state` not-supported, `ima_get_status` inactive, the operations Err — rather
than answering from whatever is there. Not covered by any of the four fixes: a rooted path a
CALLER passes in on Windows (`tpm_seal`'s output dir, an EFI variable path) — the caller's to
resolve.

**THE RULE.** A rooted path literal in `lib/` is a POSIX-only path: it sits under
`#ifndef CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN`, and the Windows arm asks the OS (getaddrinfo, the adapters' DNS
servers, the certificate store) or fails closed. Audit by grepping `lib/` AND every folded dep's
SOURCE for `"/` literals — two of the four (sandhi, sigil) were in folds, and each of the four was
found one module at a time, by whoever happened to be in it; a fifth module is likelier than not.
⚠ Wine does NOT show the class (it maps a rooted path to the unix root); only real Windows (cass)
proves it. A test plants the file itself at the exact path the old code opened, asserts the plant
exists, and removes only what it created — or, to plant every path without touching C:, maps a
scratch directory to a drive letter with `subst` and runs from that drive (sigil
`scripts/cass-rooted-paths.sh`).

**THE SAME PLANTING THROUGH THE DLL SEARCH ORDER.** An IAT import of a DLL that is not a KnownDLL
(`HKLM\...\Session Manager\KnownDLLs`: kernel32, ws2_32 and shell32 are; crypt32, dxgi and
bcryptprimitives are not) is looked for in the APPLICATION DIRECTORY first, so a `crypt32.dll`
dropped beside the program runs at its load. CVE-65's store reader therefore does not import
crypt32: it loads it from System32 at first use, with no compiler change at all —

    var k  = syscall(0xF013, "kernel32.dll");          # GetModuleHandleA
    var ll = syscall(0xF014, k, "LoadLibraryExA");      # GetProcAddress
    var c  = callptr(ll, "crypt32.dll", 0, 0x800);      # LOAD_LIBRARY_SEARCH_SYSTEM32
    var f  = syscall(0xF014, c, "CertOpenStore");        # then callptr(f, ...): 5+ args go on the stack

— and it keeps crypt32 out of the startup of every program that never verifies. Mask a BOOL /
DWORD result (`& 0xFFFFFFFF`: the upper half is undefined) and read `GetLastError` (0xF04B)
straight after the call it explains. CHANGELOG [6.6.11] [6.6.12] [6.6.14] [6.6.15]; sigil `docs/audit/2026-10-03-3.13.9-pin-bump-windows-rooted-paths-audit.md` §F8.

[[entries]]
name = "a_write_to_a_reset_peer_raises_sigpipe_and_a_library_may_neither_rely_on_nor_change_the_disposition"
description = "CLASS. write(2) to a socket whose peer has reset raises SIGPIPE, whose default action kills the WHOLE process — every connection of a server, on the say-so of any client that disconnects. An application may ignore the signal; a LIBRARY may neither assume it did nor change the process-wide disposition for it. Per OS: Linux MSG_NOSIGNAL per call; macOS SO_NOSIGPIPE per socket, failing CLOSED when xnu refuses it; a library that does not own the write (libssl) holds SIGPIPE off in the calling thread around the call. CVE-66 (6.6.14) was both TLS backends at once; lib/net.cyr's plain writers are the open instance"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["platform", "linux", "macos", "signals", "sigpipe", "sockets", "security", "seccomp", "tls", "libssl", "CVE-66", "v6.4.51", "v6.6.14"]
---
**THE TRIGGER IS ORDINARY.** The peer closes; our next write reaches a closed socket and its
kernel answers RST; the write after that is EPIPE — with SIGPIPE, unless the write said
otherwise. Measured against the 6.6.13 lib: a native TLS writer exited 141 on x86_64, pi, ecb and
ach (one of the five rows was the ClientHello, the handshake's own first write), and a libssl
SERVER died in `tls_accept_complete` answering a client that had connected and hung up.

**INSTANCES.** v6.4.51 `signal_ignore(SIGPIPE)` — right for an APPLICATION (sandhi's server
calls it), wrong inside a library. 6.6.6: a pump writing into a child that already exited dies of
it (`pollout_promises_pipe_buf_not_the_whole_write`). 6.6.14, CVE-66: every native TLS write, and
every libssl call that can write. **Open:** `lib/net.cyr`'s `sock_send` / `sock_send_all`,
`lib/http.cyr` and `lib/ws.cyr` are still flagless writes on Linux and macOS (roadmap backlog).

**THE FIX, PER MECHANISM** (`_tn_os_write` / `_tn_nosigpipe` in `lib/tls_native_conn.cyr`,
`_tls_ssl_call` in `lib/tls.cyr`):

- **Linux:** `sys_sendto(fd, buf, n, 0x4000 /*MSG_NOSIGNAL*/, 0, 0)`; on ENOTSOCK (-88) fall
  back to `write(2)` — a pipe or a file keeps its SIGPIPE, as no per-call flag exists for one.
- **macOS:** no `MSG_NOSIGNAL` in the API; `setsockopt(fd, 0xFFFF, 0x1022 /*SO_NOSIGPIPE*/)`
  before the connection's first write. ⛔ xnu REFUSES it (EINVAL) on a socket shut down both
  ways — exactly the state an RST leaves — so "set it, ignore failure, write" is the hole itself:
  any failure but ENOTSOCK (38) fails the write closed. A mutant ignoring the failure read 141 on
  ecb and ach.
- **A write you do not own** (OpenSSL's socket BIO calls `write(2)`; Linux has no per-socket
  flag): libpq's pattern, per call — `rt_sigprocmask(SIG_BLOCK, {SIGPIPE}, &old)`; if SIGPIPE was
  ALREADY blocked, note with `rt_sigpending` whether one was pending (that one is the caller's);
  make the call; if none was pending before and one is now, take it with a zero-timeout
  `rt_sigtimedwait`; restore `old`. Consume unconditionally — without errno from inside libssl you
  cannot know whether a write hit EPIPE, and a SIGPIPE left pending fires when the mask is restored.
  ~1.1 µs per call. Wrap EVERY call that can write: `SSL_read` (alerts, KeyUpdate answers),
  `SSL_connect` / `SSL_accept`, `SSL_shutdown` and 0-RTT too, not only `SSL_write`.
- Windows and agnos raise no such signal (agnos `sock_send#48` answers -1 for a dead connection).

**THREE TRAPS ON THE WAY.**
1. **The fix changes the SYSCALL SET.** A native TLS socket write is now `sendto` (44 x86_64,
   206 aarch64), macOS adds a `setsockopt`. A seccomp allowlist that admits `write` but not
   `sendto` — kavach's `basic` profile, which kills on a miss — now SIGSYS-kills the writer on its
   first record, and a fallback cannot help because a seccomp kill is not an error return. Record
   a syscall-set change as a ⚠ behaviour change whenever a stdlib leaf switches syscall.
2. **A child-process test passes vacuously under an inherited ignore** — SIG_IGN survives execve.
   The child calls `signal_default(SIGPIPE)` first; a death by signal comes back from `wait_pid`
   as 128 + signo, so 141 is the assertion. And the other way round: **GitHub's runner starts its
   steps with SIGPIPE IGNORED**, so a test that ASSERTS "still SIG_DFL" without setting it reads 1
   on CI only (6.6.14 handoff: `tls_libssl_sigpipe`). Every test sets the disposition it depends on;
   reproduce CI with `perl -e '$SIG{PIPE}="IGNORE"; exec @ARGV' ./test`.
3. **There are two write loops** (plain, and the deadline's non-blocking one): protect the LEAF
   both share and test every row with and without a deadline (gotchas
   `a_guard_inside_one_copier_covers_only_that_copier`).
4. **A forked test peer that writes its records in SEVERAL writes races the side under test**, which
   reads the first, refuses, alerts and closes: a peer descheduled between writes meets the closed
   socket. Before the fix that was a rare SIGPIPE death; after it, a `TLS_ERR_IO` and a wrong child
   exit code — CI's ARM runner hit it, a loaded pi failed 4 of 20 (`tls_native_alert_mapping`) and
   3 of 3 (`tls_native_ccs_deadline`'s write hook). Seal everything the peer sends at that point into
   ONE buffer and write it once. CHANGELOG [6.6.14] CVE-66.

[[entries]]
name = "a_winsock_socket_is_not_a_file_handle"
description = "On Windows `sys_read` / `sys_write` are ReadFile / WriteFile, and those FAIL on a Winsock SOCKET made with WSA_FLAG_OVERLAPPED (lib/net.cyr's sockets), so any stdlib leaf that bottoms out in them cannot carry a socket — native TLS did not run over a net.cyr socket on Windows at all until 6.6.14, and nothing noticed because no cross-OS test ran TLS over a socket. Use ws2_32 recv / send, and keep the file leaf as the fallback for a HANDLE. The rest of the Winsock spellings differ too"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["platform", "windows", "pe", "winsock", "sockets", "deadline", "testing", "tls", "v6.6.14"]
---
**THE LEAF** (`_tn_os_read` / `_tn_os_write`, `lib/tls_native_conn.cyr`):

    r = syscall(0xF048 /*recv*/ or 0xF047 /*send*/, fd, buf, min(n, 0x7FFFFFFF), 0)
    on failure: e = syscall(0xF024 /*WSAGetLastError*/) & 0xFFFFFFFF
      10038 WSAENOTSOCK, 10093 WSANOTINITIALISED -> sys_read / sys_write (a pipe or file HANDLE)
      anything else -> -e

Measured on cass against the 6.6.13 lib: `tls_native_connect` / `_accept` over a loopback pair
returned `TLS_ERR_IO` at once — 34 of the socket-transport test's 44 assertions failed.

**THE OTHER SPELLINGS.** Mask `WSAGetLastError` to 32 bits — only eax is defined (⚠
`lib/syscalls_windows.cyr`'s `fd_wait_ready` still returns it unmasked; backlog). Readiness is
`WSAPoll` (POLLRDNORM / POLLWRNORM), sockets only: a HANDLE answers -10038, which must route to
the between-calls fallback, not to an error. Would-block is WSAEWOULDBLOCK 10035, not EAGAIN.
Non-blocking is `ioctlsocket(FIONBIO)`, which CANNOT be read back — so a bounded write leaves
the socket blocking (the mode net.cyr creates it in) where Linux / macOS restore saved F_GETFL
flags; ⚠ and an accepted socket INHERITS a non-blocking listener's FIONBIO, so native TLS with no
deadline on it fails `TLS_ERR_IO` (backlog).

**A TEST TRAP: AFD TAKES THE WHOLE SEND.** A non-blocking `send` of 64 MiB to a peer that never
reads returned 67108864 in ONE call on cass: Windows admits a send whole while the socket's
buffered bytes are under SO_SNDBUF; only the NEXT send sees WSAEWOULDBLOCK. Linux and macOS block
after a few hundred KB. So a "fill the send buffer, expect the deadline" row writes in a loop until
a write does not complete, and asserts the ELAPSED time — once a deadline has passed every later
call answers `TLS_ERR_TIMEOUT` before any I/O, so the result code alone proves nothing.
CHANGELOG [6.6.14] (roadmap backlog j).
